# RL90 — custom controller for the PopBloom RL90 aquarium lamp

The original Tuya CBU (BK7231N) Wi-Fi module in the PopBloom RL90 is replaced by a Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3
running custom firmware: 4 PWM LED channels + fan, DS3231 RTC, 32 KB I2C FRAM, daily light programs executed
locally, web GUI with a curve editor, later ESP-NOW sync between several lamps.

Status: stage 1 implemented (backend, GUI with editor, OTA), not yet tested on hardware.

## Build, upload and monitor

Requires [PlatformIO](https://platformio.org/). Two environments in `platformio.ini`:

| Environment              | Upload                    | Notes                                   |
|--------------------------|---------------------------|-----------------------------------------|
| `seeed_xiao_esp32c3`     | USB (default, no `-e`)    | first flash, partition table changes    |
| `seeed_xiao_esp32c3_ota` | Wi-Fi, espota → 192.168.10.5 | same firmware, only the upload differs |

### OTA password (set once, before the first build)

The OTA password is baked into the firmware at build time and sent by espota on upload (`--auth`). Both come from
the shell variable `OTA_PASSWORD_192_168_10_5` — named after the device's IP (dots → underscores), not the project,
because several IoT devices share one secrets file. The name is set in `platformio.ini`
(`custom_ota_password_var`); `scripts/ota_secret.py` (pre-script) reads it, writes
`.pio/build/<env>/generated/ota_secret.h` and adds `--auth` for espota.

The password is deliberately not in `platformio.ini` via `${sysenv...}`: its value would become part of
PlatformIO's project checksum, and any difference between runs (e.g. a shell without the secrets file) would wipe
the whole `.pio/build` and recompile both environments from scratch. Now a changed (or missing) password
recompiles only `main.cpp`.

- `-t upload` with the variable unset stops before flashing (`... nie ustawione — upload przerwany`) — otherwise the
  lamp would get an empty password and every later OTA would fail with `Authentication Failed`. This applies to USB
  uploads too.
- A plain build without the variable only warns and bakes in an empty password (fine for compile checks).

Keep it permanently outside the repo:

```bash
mkdir -p ~/.secrets && chmod 700 ~/.secrets
echo "export OTA_PASSWORD_192_168_10_5='your-password'" >> ~/.secrets/iot.env   # single quotes: # $ spaces are safe
chmod 600 ~/.secrets/iot.env

# ~/.zshrc
[ -f ~/.secrets/iot.env ] && source ~/.secrets/iot.env
```

Check in the shell that runs `pio` (an export in another terminal/process does not reach it):

```bash
[ -n "$OTA_PASSWORD_192_168_10_5" ] && echo set || echo MISSING
```

### USB (serial)

```bash
pio run                                   # build only
pio run -t upload                         # build + flash over USB (XIAO native USB-CDC, usually /dev/ttyACM0)
pio run -t upload --upload-port /dev/ttyACM0
pio device monitor                        # 115200 baud, Ctrl+C to exit
pio run -t upload -t monitor              # flash and open the monitor in one go
```

The first flash of firmware containing OTA must go over USB — OTA does not exist on the device until
`ArduinoOTA.begin()` is running. A partition table change (`board_build.partitions`) also requires USB.
If the port does not show up (e.g. firmware stuck in a boot loop), hold BOOT while plugging in the cable.

### Wi-Fi (OTA upload)

```bash
pio run -e seeed_xiao_esp32c3_ota -t upload                              # lamp 192.168.10.5 (default)
pio run -e seeed_xiao_esp32c3_ota -t upload --upload-port 192.168.10.6   # lamp 192.168.10.6

# without ~/.secrets/iot.env — the variable must be in the SAME command as pio
OTA_PASSWORD_192_168_10_5='your-password' pio run -e seeed_xiao_esp32c3_ota -t upload
```

The password variable follows the target IP: `--upload-port 192.168.10.6` reads `OTA_PASSWORD_192_168_10_6`
(`scripts/ota_secret.py`). That password is both baked into the uploaded firmware and sent as `--auth`, so each
lamp needs its own entry in `~/.secrets/iot.env` (the same value is fine). A USB build uses
`custom_ota_password_var` (`OTA_PASSWORD_192_168_10_5`).

The device listens on UDP 3232; after authentication it opens a TCP connection back to the computer on a fixed
port 8266 (`--host_port`). The lamp sits in the isolated IoT VLAN (192.168.10.0/24), the computer in the
LAN (192.168.2.10), and the router blocks IoT → LAN. Without an exception espota prints
`Authenticating...OK` followed by `No response from device`.

Permanent MikroTik exception placed before the rule that blocks IoT → LAN (only IoT devices → this computer,
port 8266, where something listens only during an upload):

```
/ip firewall filter add chain=forward action=accept protocol=tcp \
    src-address=192.168.10.2-192.168.10.10 dst-address=192.168.2.10 dst-port=8266 \
    comment="OTA-lamp" place-before=[find comment="Block IOT to HOME"]
```

`Authentication Failed` instead means the password baked into the running firmware differs from
the `OTA_PASSWORD_…` variable of that IP — reflash once over USB with the variable set.

After a successful upload the lamp restarts itself with channels A–D held LOW (no full-brightness flash).

### Wi-Fi (log-socket monitor)

```bash
pio device monitor --port socket://192.168.10.5:8880
```

Mirrors everything that goes through `LOG_INFO` / `LOG_WARNING` / `LOG_ERROR` (`src/core/logging.cpp`). No
authentication, LAN only, one client at a time — a second connection is dropped. It does not show crash /
panic output (that goes raw to the UART); to diagnose a crash, including one during OTA, keep a USB monitor
open.

### Wi-Fi credentials (provisioning)

Wi-Fi SSID/password and the GUI admin password are stored encrypted in FRAM and entered through the captive
portal, not at build time. Hold the button on GPIO10 for 5 s during boot → access point `RL90-LAMP-SETUP`,
password `setup12345` → the setup page opens automatically (or http://192.168.4.1).

